1. e4 e5 {Black mirrors the centre pawn. Everything in this file is the Italian set-up for White against 1...e5.} 2. Nf3 {The knight hits e5 and gets ready to castle.} 2... Nc6 {[%name "Open Games"]} {OPEN GAMES. Black guards e5 with a piece. The other second moves are below.} (2... Nf6 {[%name "Petroff Defence"] [%tags "...Nf6; symmetrical"]} {PETROFF. Black counterattacks e4 instead of defending e5.} 3. Nxe5 d6 {First kick the knight; taking on e4 at once is a classic mistake.} (3... Nxe4 $2 4. Qe2 Qe7 (4... Nf6 $4 5. Nc6+ {The discovered check from the queen wins Black's queen.}) 5. Qxe4 d6 6. d4 {White is a clean pawn up.}) 4. Nf3 Nxe4 5. d4 d5 6. Bd3 {A calm, symmetrical position where White keeps a small edge in development.}) (2... d6 {[%name "Philidor Defence"] [%tags "...d6; solid"]} {PHILIDOR. Solid but cramped: the f8-bishop is stuck behind the d6-pawn.} 3. d4 exd4 (3... Nd7 4. Bc4 c6 {Black has to cover d5 and keep an eye on f7 before developing the bishop.}) 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 Be7 6. Bf4 O-O 7. Qd2 {White castles long next with an easy game.}) 3. Bc4 {[%name "Italian Game"]} {ITALIAN. The bishop aims at f7, the weakest point in Black's camp.} 3... Bc5 {[%name "Giuoco Piano"] [%tags "...Bc5; slow"]} {GIUOCO PIANO. The quiet game: both bishops look at the opposing f-pawn.} (3... Nf6 {[%name "Two Knights Defence"] [%tags "...Nf6; sharp"]} {TWO KNIGHTS. Black counterattacks e4 and invites Ng5.} 4. d3 {The modern, quiet choice.} (4. Ng5 {[%name "Knight Attack"]} {KNIGHT ATTACK. White goes straight for f7.} 4... d5 5. exd5 Na5 {Black gives a pawn for time and the bishop pair.} (5... Nxd5 $6 6. Nxf7 {[%name "Fried Liver Attack"]} {FRIED LIVER. The knight sacrifice drags Black's king into the open.} 6... Kxf7 7. Qf3+ Ke6 8. Nc3 {Black's king is stuck in the centre for good.}) 6. Bb5+ c6 7. dxc6 bxc6 8. Be2 h6 9. Nf3 e4 10. Ne5 {White keeps the pawn; Black has a lead in development.}) 4... Be7 (4... Bc5 5. c3 {This is the main line again.}) 5. O-O O-O 6. Re1 d6 7. a4 {A slow manoeuvring game like the main line.}) (3... Be7 {[%name "Hungarian Defence"] [%tags "...Be7; passive"]} {HUNGARIAN. Black takes the sting out of Ng5 at the cost of activity.} 4. d4 d6 5. d5 Nb8 6. Bd3 {White has space and Black has a long wait ahead.}) 4. c3 {[%name "Giuoco Pianissimo"] [%tags "c3; d3"]} {PIANISSIMO. The plan is d3, castle, Re1 and Nbd2-f1-g3, a slow build.} (4. b4 {[%name "Evans Gambit"] [%tags "b4; gambit"]} {EVANS GAMBIT. White gives a pawn to win time for c3 and d4.} 4... Bxb4 5. c3 Ba5 6. d4 exd4 7. O-O {White has a big centre and quick development for the pawn.}) 4... Nf6 5. d3 {A slow build: White keeps the centre closed and develops behind it.} 5... d6 (5... d5 $6 6. exd5 Nxd5 7. O-O {Black has opened the centre while his king is still at home.}) 6. O-O a6 {Black makes room for the bishop on a7, away from b4 and d4.} (6... O-O 7. Bb3 a6 8. Nbd2 Ba7 9. h3 {This is the same plan in another order.}) 7. a4 {Space on the queenside, and a5 is ready if Black plays ...b5.} 7... O-O 8. Re1 Ba7 9. h3 {This stops ...Ng4 and ...Bg4 before the knight leaves for f1.} 9... h6 10. Nbd2 Re8 {Both sides have finished the slow build. White's knight goes to f1 and g3, and d4 comes when it helps.} *
